<body data-page="service"><?php $nb_active = 'service'; $nb_current = '/multiple-myeloma-treatment'; include __DIR__ . '/includes/header.php'; ?>

  <main id="main">
    <section class="phero" aria-labelledby="my-title">
      <div class="wrap phero__in">
        <div>
          <ol class="crumbs"><li><a href="/">Home</a></li><li><a href="/#services">Services</a></li><li>Multiple Myeloma Treatment</li></ol>
          <h1 id="my-title" data-fx="wipe">Multiple Myeloma Treatment in <span class="grad-text">Bangalore</span></h1>
          <p class="phero__sub" data-fx="fade" style="--d:.15s">Multiple myeloma is a blood cancer that starts in plasma cells, a type of white blood cell in the bone marrow. It can weaken bones, damage kidneys and lower your blood counts. Myeloma is not usually curable, but it is very treatable today, and many people live active lives for years.</p>
          <div class="hero__acts" data-fx="fade" style="--d:.25s">
            <a class="btn btn--grad" href="/contact?topic=multiple-myeloma">Book a consultation<svg class="ic ic--go"><use href="#i-go"/></svg></a>
            <a class="btn btn--line" href="tel:+917899756677"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-phone"/></svg>+91 78997 56677</a>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="phero__pic phero__pic--art" data-fx="iris" style="--d:.15s">
          <img src="/assets/images/illustrations/myeloma-cells.svg" alt="Illustration of plasma cells in the bone marrow and a bone, representing multiple myeloma" width="400" height="400" fetchpriority="high">
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="sec" aria-labelledby="my-what">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="split split--top">
          <div>
            <p class="tag" data-fx="wipe-x">Overview</p>
            <h2 class="title" id="my-what" data-fx="wipe">What is <span class="grad-text">multiple myeloma?</span></h2>
            <p class="lead" data-fx="fade">Your bone marrow makes plasma cells. Healthy plasma cells make antibodies that fight infections. In multiple myeloma, one plasma cell turns abnormal and starts making copies of itself. These myeloma cells crowd out healthy blood cells in the bone marrow.</p>
            <p class="lead" data-fx="fade">Myeloma cells also release a faulty antibody called M-protein (monoclonal protein). M-protein does not fight infection. It can build up in the blood and urine and damage the kidneys. It is called “multiple” myeloma because it often affects many bones at the same time, such as the spine, skull, ribs and hips.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="box" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><h3><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-users"/></svg>Who gets myeloma?</h3><p class="box__sub">Myeloma usually affects people over 50. It is slightly more common in men than in women, and it can run in families, though rarely.</p><p class="box__sub">About 19,400 people in India were diagnosed with multiple myeloma in 2022 (National Cancer Registry Programme, ICMR). Early signs like back pain and tiredness are often mistaken for age or a “weak back”. With timely multiple myeloma treatment in Bangalore, the disease can be controlled before it harms your bones and kidneys.</p></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="sec sec--tint" aria-labelledby="my-sym">
      <div class="wrap"><div class="head">
          <div><p class="tag" data-fx="wipe-x">Signs &amp; symptoms</p><h2 class="title" id="my-sym" data-fx="wipe">Common signs of <span class="grad-text">multiple myeloma</span></h2></div>
          <p class="lead" data-fx="fade">Early myeloma may cause no symptoms and is sometimes found on a routine blood test. Doctors remember the main signs with the word CRAB:</p>
        </div><div class="dtable-wrap" data-fx="fade"><table class="dtable"><thead><tr><th>Letter</th><th>Stands for</th><th>What you may notice</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label="Letter"><b>C</b></td><td data-label="Stands for">High calcium</td><td data-label="What you may notice">Thirst, passing urine often, constipation, confusion, nausea</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Letter"><b>R</b></td><td data-label="Stands for">Renal (kidney) damage</td><td data-label="What you may notice">Swelling in the legs, less urine, foamy urine, high creatinine</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Letter"><b>A</b></td><td data-label="Stands for">Anemia</td><td data-label="What you may notice">Tiredness, weakness, breathlessness, pale skin</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Letter"><b>B</b></td><td data-label="Stands for">Bone damage</td><td data-label="What you may notice">Back, rib or hip pain, bones that break easily</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="mt"><ul class="sym sym--3"><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-bone"/></svg><b>Back pain</b><span>That does not go away with rest or painkillers</span></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-thermo"/></svg><b>Frequent infections</b><span>Like pneumonia or urinary infections</span></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-scale"/></svg><b>Weight loss</b><span>Without trying</span></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-adult"/></svg><b>Numbness or tingling</b><span>In the hands and feet</span></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-drop"/></svg><b>Easy bruising</b><span>Or bleeding</span></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-eye"/></svg><b>Blurred vision</b><span>Headaches or dizziness (from thick blood)</span></li></ul></div><p class="alert" data-fx="fade"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-heart"/></svg><span><b>See a doctor urgently</b> if you have sudden severe back pain, weakness or numbness in the legs, trouble passing urine or stools, confusion, or very little urine. These can be signs of spinal cord pressure or kidney failure, which need emergency care.</span></p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="sec" aria-labelledby="my-cause">
      <div class="wrap"><div class="cols2">
          <div class="box" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><h3 id="my-cause"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-flask"/></svg>What causes myeloma and who is at risk?</h3><p class="box__sub">The exact cause is not known. It starts with changes (mutations) in the DNA of a plasma cell. It is not contagious, and it is not caused by anything you ate or did. The risk is higher for:</p><ul class="list"><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>People over 50, and especially over 60</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Men, who get myeloma slightly more often than women</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>People with MGUS (monoclonal gammopathy of undetermined significance), a harmless-looking condition that can slowly turn into myeloma</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>People with a parent, brother or sister who had myeloma or MGUS</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>People who are overweight</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>People with long-term exposure to radiation, pesticides, benzene or some industrial chemicals</span></li></ul><p class="box__sub">Having a risk factor does not mean you will get myeloma. Most people with MGUS never develop it, but they need a blood test once or twice a year.</p></div>
          <div class="box" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.08s"><h3><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-shield"/></svg>What happens if myeloma is left untreated?</h3><p class="box__sub">Active myeloma keeps growing without treatment. Over time it can cause:</p><ul class="list"><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Bone fractures:</b> weak spots (lytic lesions) in the spine can collapse, causing severe pain and loss of height.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Spinal cord pressure:</b> a collapsed bone can press on nerves and cause leg weakness or paralysis.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Kidney failure:</b> M-protein and high calcium can damage the kidneys, sometimes needing dialysis.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Serious infections:</b> low healthy antibodies make pneumonia and other infections more likely and more dangerous.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Severe anemia and bleeding problems</b> that affect energy and daily life.</span></li></ul><p class="box__sub">The good news: when myeloma is treated early, much of this damage can be prevented, and kidney function often improves.</p></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="sec sec--tint" aria-labelledby="my-types">
      <div class="wrap"><div class="head head--center">
          <div><p class="tag" data-fx="wipe-x">Conditions</p><h2 class="title" id="my-types" data-fx="wipe">Types of myeloma and plasma cell disorders <span class="grad-text">we treat</span></h2></div>
          
        </div><div class="cards3"><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-flask"/></svg><h3>MGUS</h3><p>A small amount of M-protein with no symptoms. Not cancer, but needs regular blood tests.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-clock"/></svg><h3>Smoldering myeloma</h3><p>More abnormal plasma cells than MGUS, but no organ damage yet. Watched closely; some high-risk cases are treated early.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-cells"/></svg><h3>Active (symptomatic) myeloma</h3><p>Myeloma that is causing CRAB problems or is likely to soon. Needs treatment.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-drop"/></svg><h3>Light chain myeloma</h3><p>Myeloma cells make only part of the antibody (light chains). More likely to affect the kidneys.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-doc"/></svg><h3>Non-secretory myeloma</h3><p>A rare type that makes little or no M-protein, so it is harder to spot on blood tests.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-bone"/></svg><h3>Solitary plasmacytoma</h3><p>A single tumour of plasma cells in one bone or soft tissue. Often treated with radiation.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-thermo"/></svg><h3>Plasma cell leukemia</h3><p>A rare, fast-growing form where myeloma cells spill into the blood. Needs urgent treatment.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-heart"/></svg><h3>AL amyloidosis</h3><p>A related condition where light chains form deposits in the heart, kidneys or nerves.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-shield"/></svg><h3>Relapsed or refractory myeloma</h3><p>Myeloma that comes back or does not respond to earlier treatment. Newer drugs and therapies can help.</p></div></div><div class="split split--top mt">
          <div class="box" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><h3><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-award"/></svg>Stages of multiple myeloma</h3><p class="box__sub">Doctors use the Revised International Staging System (R-ISS). It looks at blood tests (beta-2 microglobulin, albumin and LDH) and genetic changes in the myeloma cells. Your stage and risk group help Dr. Neema Bhat choose how strong your treatment should be.</p></div>
          <div class="dtable-wrap" data-fx="fade"><table class="dtable"><thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label="Stage"><b>Stage I</b></td><td data-label="What it means">Lower amount of disease and no high-risk genetic changes. Usually the best outlook.</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Stage"><b>Stage II</b></td><td data-label="What it means">Neither Stage I nor Stage III.</td></tr><tr><td data-label="Stage"><b>Stage III</b></td><td data-label="What it means">Higher amount of disease, plus high-risk genetic changes or high LDH. Needs more intensive treatment.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="sec" aria-labelledby="my-dx">
      <div class="wrap"><div class="head">
          <div><p class="tag" data-fx="wipe-x">Diagnosis</p><h2 class="title" id="my-dx" data-fx="wipe">How is multiple myeloma <span class="grad-text">diagnosed?</span></h2></div>
          <p class="lead" data-fx="fade">Myeloma is diagnosed with a mix of blood, urine, bone marrow and imaging tests. These tests also give a baseline, so your doctor can see how well treatment is working. Dr. Neema Bhat may advise:</p>
        </div><ol class="olist"><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><span class="olist__n">01</span><div><b>Complete Blood Count (CBC)</b><p>Checks for anemia and low blood counts.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><span class="olist__n">02</span><div><b>Kidney tests and calcium level</b><p>Serum creatinine and calcium.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><span class="olist__n">03</span><div><b>SPEP and immunofixation</b><p>Serum protein electrophoresis finds and measures M-protein in the blood.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><span class="olist__n">04</span><div><b>Serum free light chain test</b><p>Measures kappa and lambda light chains.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><span class="olist__n">05</span><div><b>Urine tests (24-hour protein, UPEP)</b><p>Check for light chains in the urine (Bence Jones protein).</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><span class="olist__n">06</span><div><b>Beta-2 microglobulin, albumin and LDH</b><p>Used for staging.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><span class="olist__n">07</span><div><b>Bone marrow aspiration and biopsy</b><p>Confirms the diagnosis and counts the plasma cells.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><span class="olist__n">08</span><div><b>FISH and genetic tests</b><p>Show whether the myeloma is standard-risk or high-risk.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><span class="olist__n">09</span><div><b>Imaging</b><p>PET-CT, whole-body low-dose CT or MRI finds bone damage that a simple X-ray may miss.</p></div></li></ol>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="sec sec--tint" aria-labelledby="my-tx">
      <div class="wrap"><div class="head">
          <div><p class="tag" data-fx="wipe-x">Treatment</p><h2 class="title" id="my-tx" data-fx="wipe">Multiple myeloma treatment <span class="grad-text">options</span></h2></div>
          <p class="lead" data-fx="fade">Your multiple myeloma treatment in Bangalore depends on your stage, risk group, age, kidney function and overall health. Most patients follow these steps:</p>
        </div><ol class="olist"><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><span class="olist__n">01</span><div><b>Induction (first) treatment</b><p>3 to 6 months of drug combinations to bring the myeloma under control.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><span class="olist__n">02</span><div><b>Stem cell transplant</b><p>For patients who are fit enough, to deepen the response.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><span class="olist__n">03</span><div><b>Maintenance</b><p>A lighter, long-term medicine to keep the myeloma from coming back.</p></div></li><li data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><span class="olist__n">04</span><div><b>Regular follow-up</b><p>Blood tests to catch any return early.</p></div></li></ol><p class="lead mt" data-fx="fade" style="max-width:none">Dr. Neema Bhat creates a plan for you that may include:</p><div class="cards3"><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-pill"/></svg><h3>Combination drug therapy</h3><p>Usually three or four medicines together, such as bortezomib, lenalidomide and dexamethasone. Many are tablets or small injections under the skin, given as outpatient care.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-pill"/></svg><h3>Targeted antibody therapy</h3><p>Medicines like daratumumab that attach to myeloma cells and help the immune system destroy them.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-marrow"/></svg><h3>Autologous stem cell transplant</h3><p>Your own stem cells are collected, you receive high-dose chemotherapy, and the cells are returned to rebuild healthy bone marrow. It helps many patients stay in remission longer. <a href="/bone-marrow-transplant">Learn more</a></p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-clock"/></svg><h3>Maintenance therapy</h3><p>Usually a daily tablet such as lenalidomide after transplant or first treatment.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-award"/></svg><h3>Newer treatments for relapse</h3><p>Next-generation drugs, bispecific antibodies and CAR-T cell therapy, where suitable and available.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-radiation"/></svg><h3>Radiation therapy</h3><p>For painful bone lesions, spinal cord pressure or a single plasmacytoma.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-pill"/></svg><h3>Bone-strengthening medicines</h3><p>Monthly zoledronic acid or denosumab to reduce fractures and bone pain.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.06s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-heart"/></svg><h3>Supportive care</h3><p>Pain relief, treatment for high calcium, kidney protection, transfusions when needed, infection prevention and vaccines.</p></div><div class="card" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.12s"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><h3>Watch and wait</h3><p>For MGUS and most smoldering myeloma, with regular blood tests and no treatment until it is needed.</p></div></div><p class="alert alert--warn" data-fx="fade"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-shield"/></svg><span><b>Myeloma care works best when started early.</b> Do not delay treatment while trying only painkillers, calcium tablets or home remedies for back pain.</span></p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="sec" aria-labelledby="my-live">
      <div class="wrap"><div class="box" data-fx="fade" style="--d:0.00s"><h3 id="my-live"><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-sun"/></svg>Living well with multiple myeloma</h3><p class="box__sub">Good daily habits support your treatment and protect your bones and kidneys:</p><ul class="list"><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Drink enough water:</b> about 2 to 3 litres a day, unless your doctor limits fluids. This protects the kidneys.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Avoid painkillers like ibuprofen and diclofenac (NSAIDs):</b> they can harm the kidneys. Ask your doctor which pain medicine is safe.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Eat a balanced Indian diet:</b> dals, eggs, paneer, curd, fish or chicken for protein; fruits and vegetables for vitamins. Wash raw foods well, as infections are more likely during treatment.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Do not take calcium or herbal supplements without asking your doctor, as calcium may already be high.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Stay gently active:</b> walking and light exercise keep you strong, but avoid heavy lifting and sudden twisting to protect the spine.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span><b>Prevent infections:</b> wash hands often, avoid crowds when your counts are low, and take the vaccines your doctor advises (flu, pneumonia, shingles, COVID-19).</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Visit a dentist before starting bone-strengthening injections, and keep good dental hygiene.</span></li><li><svg class="ic"><use href="#i-check"/></svg><span>Keep every follow-up appointment, even when you feel well.</span></li></ul><p class="box__sub">Multiple myeloma cannot be prevented. But people with MGUS or a family history can get regular blood tests so that any change is found early.</p></div>
      </div>
    </section>

