        <div class="faq2__list"><details class="qa" open><summary><span class="qa__n">01</span><span class="qa__q">Which doctor should I see for multiple myeloma in Bangalore?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">A hematologist or hemato-oncologist (blood cancer specialist) treats multiple myeloma. Dr. Neema Bhat is a US-trained hematologist in Bangalore and bone marrow transplant specialist who treats myeloma and related plasma cell disorders.</p></details><details class="qa"><summary><span class="qa__n">02</span><span class="qa__q">Is multiple myeloma curable?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">Multiple myeloma is usually not curable, but it is very treatable. Many patients go into remission and stay well for years with modern medicines, stem cell transplant and maintenance therapy.</p></details><details class="qa"><summary><span class="qa__n">03</span><span class="qa__q">What is the life expectancy with multiple myeloma?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">It depends on the stage, genetic risk, age and response to treatment. With today’s treatments, many people live 7 to 10 years or longer, and some live much longer.</p></details><details class="qa"><summary><span class="qa__n">04</span><span class="qa__q">What is the first sign of multiple myeloma?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">The most common early sign is bone pain, especially in the back or ribs. Tiredness from anemia, frequent infections and kidney problems are also common early clues.</p></details><details class="qa"><summary><span class="qa__n">05</span><span class="qa__q">Is a bone marrow transplant needed for myeloma?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">Not for everyone. An autologous stem cell transplant (using your own cells) is advised for patients who are fit enough, because it helps keep myeloma in remission longer. Your doctor will tell you if it suits you.</p></details><details class="qa"><summary><span class="qa__n">06</span><span class="qa__q">How long does myeloma treatment take?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">First treatment usually takes 3 to 6 months. If a transplant is planned, it follows soon after. Maintenance therapy then continues, often for 2 years or more.</p></details><details class="qa"><summary><span class="qa__n">07</span><span class="qa__q">Can myeloma come back after treatment?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">Yes, myeloma often returns after some time. That is why regular follow-up tests matter. If it comes back, there are many effective treatment options.</p></details><details class="qa"><summary><span class="qa__n">08</span><span class="qa__q">What is the difference between MGUS and myeloma?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">MGUS is a harmless-looking condition with a small amount of M-protein and no symptoms. Myeloma has more abnormal cells and causes damage to bones, kidneys or blood. About 1 in 100 people with MGUS develop myeloma or a related disease each year.</p></details><details class="qa"><summary><span class="qa__n">09</span><span class="qa__q">Is multiple myeloma hereditary?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">Myeloma is not directly inherited. Having a close family member with myeloma or MGUS slightly raises your risk, but most patients have no family history.</p></details><details class="qa"><summary><span class="qa__n">10</span><span class="qa__q">How much does multiple myeloma treatment in Bangalore cost?</span><span class="qa__pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><p class="qa__a">The cost depends on the drugs used, the number of cycles and whether a stem cell transplant is needed. The clinic can share an estimate after your first consultation and help you with insurance paperwork.</p></details></div>
